#!/bin/sh
#
# PRE-PUSH: the whole gate runs before anything leaves this machine.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, written into the hook so it cannot be deleted as unexplained.
# On 13 August 2026 the integration branch was found with five failing tests
# that nobody had ever seen, because `npm test` had never been run on it. One
# of them, tests/unit/security/rls-column-exposure.test.ts, had never executed
# AT ALL: a shebang on the module it imports made the file fail to collect,
# vitest reported "no tests", and the suite passed vacuously. Three of the five
# arrived AFTER the merges, on commits pushed straight to the branch, so the gap
# was not "run the suite before merging" but "run it before pushing", which is
# what a pre-push hook is for.
#
# On 15 August a type error reached the remote and put two preview deployments
# into ERROR after `tsc --noEmit` HAD been run, HAD exited 0 and was quoted as
# evidence: it ran before the final edit and was quoted after it. A hook cannot
# stop somebody quoting an old measurement; it can make the last gate before
# code leaves the machine measure the tree that is actually leaving.
#
# On 29 August the hook and CI were found to disagree: lint ran in CI and not
# here, and eight warnings had been red on CI since before that session began
# while every push looked clean from this machine.
#
# On 5 September 2026 six failed-run emails arrived for ONE pull request,
# because CI was still the first place four of its checks ever ran. The
# close-out of that day made it law: nothing is pushed until the SAME checks
# pass locally, all of them, as one command.
#
# WHAT RUNS is defined in ONE place, scripts/ops/pre-push-gate.mjs, which is
# also `npm run gate:push`: the typecheck, lint, the copy gate, the
# critical-path guard, the exemption clock, every registered guard, the
# types-drift guard, the suite through the test-count canary, the build, and
# the Lighthouse mobile gate on that build served locally. This file only hands
# the gate the ref list git gives a pre-push hook on stdin and returns its
# verdict. Nothing is selected or skipped here: a flag on the line below is
# exactly what scripts/guards/pre-push-gate-wired.mjs fails the build over.
#
# BYPASS. `git push --no-verify` skips this, and that is deliberate: a hook
# that cannot be bypassed in an emergency is a hook someone deletes. Bypassing
# it is a decision to push unchecked code, so make it knowingly.
#
# The hook does not choose a Node; it uses whatever is on PATH, and the guard
# registry reports whether that matches the .nvmrc contract (Law 9).

node scripts/ops/pre-push-gate.mjs
status=$?

if [ $status -ne 0 ]; then
  echo ""
  echo "[pre-push] BLOCKED: the gate did not pass, so nothing was pushed."
  echo "[pre-push] Read the step it stopped at above. Bypassing with --no-verify"
  echo "[pre-push] is a decision to share unchecked code."
fi

exit $status
